`browser.start` now accepts `edge` (or `msedge`) and launches an isolated Microsoft Edge pointed at the proxy. I didn't run the tests, as instructed.

- **`mitmproxy/addons/browser.py`:**
  - New `launch_edge()` looks for Edge in these places, in order:
    - the macOS app bundle;
    - the two Windows `Program Files` paths;
    - Linux binaries `microsoft-edge`, `-stable`, `-beta`, `-dev` and `msedge`;
    - the `com.microsoft.Edge` flatpak.
  - Edge is Chromium-based, so it reuses the same launch flags as Chrome (temp `--user-data-dir`, `--proxy-server`, and the no-first-run options). I moved the Chrome launch code into a shared `_launch_chromium_based(cmd)` helper. That helper also keeps the "platform is not supported" message when nothing is found, so Chrome's behaviour is unchanged.
- **`test/mitmproxy/addons/test_browser.py`:** added a test that Edge starts and one that a missing Edge logs "platform is not supported".
- **`CHANGELOG.md`:** added an entry.
